ng those  service members for their service but i  think some of the biggest  news moments of the speech came in the  question and answer session  that the president held afterwards  because  on the one hand he talked about how his  secretary of state anthony blinken  will be continuing diplomatic efforts to  see that there is a negotiated  peace effort in the region but at the  same time saying that one of the parties  the taliban that is a part of that  effort the u.s president was asked  whether or not he trusted them and he  was very emphatic when he said  no he does not so it almost appears as  if he's undermined that process  from the podium the other big headline  in all of that  is that the u.s president has been very  clear that despite  dire predictions from security experts  members of congress former members of  the administration  passed there have been these predictions  that the taliban  will in light of the gains that have  been made take  over potentially kabul other major  cities the u.s president emphatic in  saying that he does not believe that  will happen  that this is because of the training  that has been provided by the united  states  the ongoing support that will be  provided and also the enormous amount of  money and investment that the united  states has made  so the u.s president commander in chief  striking an optimistic tone  even as there are grave concerns about  the future of  afghanistan kimberly hawking thank you  so much for  that a wrap-up from washington d.c let's  bring in once again david sydney who's a  former deputy assistant secretary of  defense for afghanistan pakistan  and central asia thanks so much for  sticking around uh just to pick up on  something important that kimberly was  mentioning mentioning a moment ago  why do you think the president has now  said that the military mission  will conclude on august 31st as opposed  to september 11th which was the original  date well the administration has gotten  a lot of 